# Build context for the shared image. The Dockerfile copies pyproject.toml,
# src/, examples/, and the two documents the guide example reads — so
# everything else is context transfer with no build output.

# Secrets. The API key is supplied at run time via env_file, never baked in.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Version control and tooling metadata
.git
.gitignore
.dockerignore
.github
.vscode
.idea

# Python build and cache artefacts
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.venv*/
venv/
build/
dist/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/

# Not needed to build or run the image. `db/schema.sql` is mounted into the
# postgres container, not into this one; tests run on the host.
research/
tests/
db/
docs/

# The frontend. It builds from its own context (./ui) with its own Dockerfile
# and never enters the Python image, so `ui/` alone is enough — the two paths
# after it are spelled out anyway, because they are the ones that hurt if a
# later edit narrows this rule: a populated node_modules is tens of thousands
# of files and hundreds of megabytes of context transfer for a build that would
# not read one byte of it, and dist/ is build output masquerading as source.
ui/
ui/node_modules/
ui/dist/

Makefile
docker-compose.*.yml
Dockerfile

# Root-level markdown only. Docker matches a bare `*.md` against single path
# components, so this leaves nested files alone.
#
# The two re-includes are not tidying. The guide example answers *from* the
# README and reads the service list out of the compose file, and it derives the
# set of real identifiers from both — which is what its output guardrail checks
# an answer against. Excluded, the agent still boots, still looks healthy, and
# answers every question with "not documented".
*.md
!README.md
!docker-compose.yml
